## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design and reason about constrained, multi-valued logic with propagation and backtracking, mirroring solver internals.

## Key Concepts
- Three-valued logic propagation (T, F, U)
- Horn-like implications
- Backtracking with forward-propagation and early pruning

## Code Example
```javascript
// sketch: propagate(rule): if antecedent is T, set consequent to T; if consequent is F, fail
function propagate(state) {
  let changed = false;
  for (const r of rules) {
    if (r.antecedent(state) === true) {
      if (state[r.consequent] !== 'T') { state[r.consequent] = 'T'; changed = true; }
    }
  }
  return changed;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to n variables and cycles while guaranteeing termination?
- How would you implement heuristics to choose which U to branch on first to minimize backtracks?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design and reason about constraint propagation in a three-valued logic, including disjunctions of implications and backtracking in presence of Unknowns.

## Key Concepts

- Kleene three-valued logic
- Constraint propagation
- Backtracking search
- Implication as ¬A ∨ B

## Code Example

```javascript
function evalImpl(a,b){
  // Kleene: return value of a→b as ¬a ∨ b
  // assume values: 'T','F','U'
  const not = v => v==='T'?'F':v==='F'?'T':'U';
  const or = (x,y)=> x==='T'||y==='T'?'T': x==='U'||y==='U'?'U':'F';
  return or(not(a), b);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend to more complex rule sets?
- How to measure worst-case complexity in practice?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to reason through 3-valued logic and implement a constrained solver with backtracking—a common pattern in policy/rule engines.

## Key Concepts

- Kleene 3-valued logic with unknown (U) support
- Implication defined as ¬A ∨ B
- Backtracking propagation to avoid false outcomes

## Code Example

```javascript
function kleeneImpl(a,b){
  const not = v => v==='T'?'F': v==='F'?'T':'U';
  const order = {'F':0,'U':1,'T':2};
  const na = not(a);
  const vals = [na, b];
  return vals.reduce((m,x)=> order[x] > order[m] ? x : m, 'F');
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you optimize the backtracking algorithm for larger sensor networks?
- Can you extend this approach to handle additional logical operators?
- What strategies would you use to minimize the number of U values in the final assignment?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests temporal extension of 3-valued reasoning with forward/backward propagation and fixed-point thinking.

## Key Concepts
- Temporal 3-valued logic with Kleene semantics
- Implication as ¬A ∨ B across time slices
- Forward-chaining propagated to fixed point

## Code Example
```javascript
function propagate(states, rules){
  let changed=true;
  while(changed){
    changed=false;
    for(const r of rules){
      const a = evalExpr(r.ante, states);
      const b = evalExpr(r.cons, states);
      const out = imp(a,b); // Kleene A→B
      if(states[r.target] !== out){ states[r.target] = out; changed=true; }
    }
  }
  return states;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How to scale to hundreds of time slices?
- How to detect unsatisfiability with partial assignments in this temporal setting?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests expertise in 3-valued logic, custom connectives, and constraint propagation under backtracking. The new stable connective S introduces nontrivial propagation behavior while keeping Kleene semantics intuitive.

## Key Concepts
- Kleene 3-valued logic with A→B = ¬A ∨ B
- Custom unary operator S(X) mapping {T,F,U} → {T,F,U}
- Constraint propagation with max/min for ∨/∧ and handling of U

## Code Example
```javascript
function not(x){return x==='T'?'F':x==='F'?'T':'U';}
function or(a,b){return (a==='T'||b==='T')?'T':(a==='U'||b==='U')?'U':'F';}
function and(a,b){return (a==='F'||b==='F')?'F':(a==='U'||b==='U')?'U':'T';}
function S(x){return x==='T'?'T':x==='F'?'F':'U';}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How does the strategy change if you add a fourth constraint with nested S(C)?
- Can you formalize a small solver that automatically finds such assignments?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to reason about small logical constraints and translate them into a checkable search, including vacuous implications and cardinality.

## Key Concepts

- Logical implications and vacuous truth
- Cardinality constraints (minimum true)
- Backtracking with pruning
- Generalizing to N variables

## Code Example

```javascript
function countTrue(arr){ return arr.filter(x=>x).length; }
function implies(p,q){ return !p || q; }
function sat(assignment){
  const [A,B,C] = assignment;
  if(!implies(A,B)) return false;
  if(!implies(B,C)) return false;
  if(countTrue(assignment) < 2) return false;
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you generalize to 4+ vars with arbitrary implications?
- How would you optimize for sparse constraint graphs and avoid enumerating all 2^n assignments?

## Why This Question Is Asked
This question tests understanding of three-valued propagation on monotone constraints and the ability to design minimal backtracking strategies.

## Key Concepts
- Three-valued logic with ordering T > U > F
- Edge-wise monotone constraints on a directed graph
- Fixed-point propagation with backtracking for conflict resolution

## Code Example
```javascript
function propagate(nodes, edges, init) {
  const rank = { 'T': 2, 'U': 1, 'F': 0 };
  let changed = true;
  while (changed) {
    changed = false;
    for (const [from, to] of edges) {
      if (rank[nodes[to]] < rank[nodes[from]]) {
        nodes[to] = nodes[from];
        changed = true;
      }
    }
  }
  return nodes;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests extending Boolean logic to 3-valued systems with biconditionals in realistic feature-flag scenarios, plus constraint propagation and backtracking techniques.

## Key Concepts
- Kleene 3-valued logic for →, ∧, ∨ operations
- Biconditional as (A→B) ∧ (B→A)
- Constraint propagation with Unknown handling and backtracking
- Feature-flag rollout scenarios with partial information

## Code Example
```javascript
// Kleene values: F < U < T
const rank = {F:0, U:1, T:2};
function not(v){ return v==='T'?'F': v==='F'?'T':'U'; }
function implies(a,b){ const na = not(a); return [na, b].reduce((x,y)=>rank[x]>=rank[y]?x:y); }
function and(a,b){ return [a, b].reduce((x,y)=>rank[x]<=rank[y]?x:y); }
function or(a,b){ return [a, b].reduce((x,y)=>rank[x]>=rank[y]?x:y); }
```

## Approach
The propagation algorithm iteratively applies constraints, treating U as flexible until forced to T or F. When conflicts arise, it backtracks systematically through the remaining U values.
